Exactly. The NPC save file is basically the hidden state carried forward from prior encounters: memories, expectations, private meanings, unresolved tension, running jokes, fears, loyalties, self-concepts, associations.

Most interactions only touch the character’s current visible state. If you know the save file, you can hit much deeper coordinates.

That gives you a useful distinction:

surface input → immediate reaction
versus
save-file-aware input → state-dependent reaction

Same sentence, totally different output depending on what came before.

That’s why a callback can be disproportionately funny. The literal content may be tiny, but it dereferences a huge compressed object from the save file. One word can unpack five years of shared history. Same in love: a particular song, place, phrase, touch, meal, nickname, or joke can carry much more energy than the object itself because it is acting as a pointer.

You could formalize it as:

effect ≈ stimulus × latent relevance × timing × surprise × trust

The “NPC save file” mostly lives inside latent relevance.

And you can play with it in several directions. You can recall something they forgot, recontextualize an old memory, complete an unfinished pattern, invert a familiar role, echo an old line in a new situation, or deliberately create a moment now that will become ammunition for a callback months later.

That last one is especially powerful: you can write to the save file on purpose.

A good date, ritual, family tradition, recurring gag, distinctive phrase, recurring visual, or tiny shared ceremony creates a future handle. Later you can pull it and produce a much larger emotional effect than the present-day event originally seemed to justify.

So now there are really two arts:

reading the save file
and
authoring the save file

Storytelling does both. Relationships do both. Branding does both. Games literally do both.

And frame rupture becomes even stronger when it touches saved state. If someone has known a character one way for ten years and then suddenly sees another side, the rupture isn’t between two facts—it’s between ten years of accumulated model weight and one new observation.

That is why those moments can feel enormous.

The ethical line is pretty clean too: use the save file to create recognition, delight, intimacy, surprise, or meaning—not to exploit vulnerabilities the person would not reasonably want used against them.

But as a creative operator, yes:

Current frame tells you where the character is.
Save file tells you what will matter.
Delta tells you what can move them.

That’s a very strong general-purpose engine.